ndoff just for context is an  iOS feature where you can pass data  between your running applications and  generally it's for going from iOS to Mac  or iOS to iOS or vice versa but perhaps  a little-known feature of handoff is  that you can actually handoff from a  website from your domain to your native  application and vice versa so that if  someone is using your web app and they  pull up their phone they can actually  get a handoff recommendation says Oh  open this in my app and vice versa they  can be using your app on their phone and  then get a handoff recommendation on  their laptop that says go to this web  URL and that is pretty cool I think I  don't know why more apps don't use that  but we can imagine we could do this in  middleware without changing any of the  application logic code at all if we have  this what here is an imaginary custom  that we wrote just pretend this API  exists it basically looks like this and  are in our hand off middleware we could  you know subscribe to a hook that says  okay when we get a hand off event we get  some data in this case handing off from  the web will just get a URL that's the  only thing we'll get unfortunately but  we should be able to take that URL and  transform it into some navigation state  in our application which we can do just  by in our case we have all of our  navigation state inside of Redux but if  you were using some sort of navigator  pattern you could also just call  functions on the navigator you know do  whatever you need to do to restore that  URL presumably you have an analogue to  that URL state in your native  application and then going the other way  you can observe either you know methods  on your navigator hook into those calls  or observe navigation actions if you're  just dispatching navigation actions and  let your the handoff native module know  this is this is a URL you know you build  a URL that represents the current  navigation state and say that's the  current URL I would want the user to  resume - if they chose to do so  and that's it we didn't change any of  the web code we didn't write wrap any  actions or anything like that so use  middleware it's great cool  so in summary this has been a pretty big  success for us so far you might look at  the thunk adapter thing and say that's  crazy I can't believe you're doing that  and that it's a heck it probably is  that's okay it's working for us and  we're shipping a lot of code on mobile  really fast  so that's valuable some of the  challenges are keeping up to date with  the web projects there's a lot of things  that aren't caught in that compile step  that only get caught at runtime like  right now if they added a new if someone  again went into a reducer and said local  storage docket item it would not blow up  until we pulled in the new version of  the package and then we would have to go  back and fix it and then do it again so  that's kind of an annoying thing and is  just kind of a manual process of talking  to the teams and seeing what they're  working on so that could be better also  training I don't know if you guys have  found this  training people in react and redux if  they are not familiar with functional  programming is hard it takes a little  bit longer than I would assume it does  and that's especially true for remember  our excited mobile developers who were  thrilled to be riding with Jas before  it's especially true for them because if  you've been in that world that's a very  object oriented this is very very  different  and it is hard for them to learn they're  smart people obviously but it's just a  hard thing it's a complete paradigm  shift and so that is a challenge not  insurmountable but is a challenge some  future things that we would like to do  have automatic pull requests when new  packages are deployed this is just kind  of like a nice logistical thing that  could be great and this means we could  also write integration tests with their  thunks and make sure that everything  works right  then there's acceptance testing going  one step further acceptance testing on  mobile is hard because there at least  with react native there isn't a way to  do this headless Lee you have to use a  simulator or you'd have to go like use  react native web and render your mobile  application in a webview and then use  the web tools to do acceptance testing  which is seems to me too much work to  maintain just to get acceptance testing  on your mobile application so you're  stuck with like running in simulator and  using those api's which is hard that's  just a challenge but something that we  would like to solve and like I mentioned  before catching more things at bundle  time caching references to window  objects or Dom API is that or SVG's  things that we don't that are going to  fail at runtime we'd like to move that  catch to compile time if possible and  and then lastly I'm going to go next  door and learn about how I should  replace Redux with rxjs because  apparently that's the new hotness that's  it for me and a little bit early please  if you have questions I really want to  talk about all this stuff and if there  are better ways to do it please come  tell me if you're doing this I would  love to hear about that thing  to you very much  [Applause]


